          <h2>On the M6 north of Birmingham</h2>
          <p>Walsall sits directly on the M6 as it continues north from <a href="birmingham.html">Birmingham</a> and <a href="west-bromwich.html">West Bromwich</a> towards Staffordshire, served by its own junctions rather than a single interchange, and carries a mix of local traffic, commuters and longer journeys further up the M6. A good number of the breakdowns we attend around Walsall happen on this stretch of motorway rather than in the town itself — if that's your situation, your direction of travel and the nearest junction number give us what we need to plan the route in.</p>

          <h2>A market town at the centre of a wider borough</h2>
          <p>The town itself still holds a working street market and serves as the hub for a wide surrounding area, meaning many of the vehicles we recover in Walsall have come in from smaller neighbourhoods such as Aldridge and Rushall rather than starting their journey in the town centre. If you've broken down somewhere outside the centre, a neighbourhood name, village or the nearest crossroads is far more useful to us than a postcode alone.</p>

          <h2>The edge of the Black Country</h2>
          <p>Walsall marks something of a boundary — part Black Country, part the greener fringe towards Staffordshire — and the roads on its northern and eastern edges reflect that, with quieter residential streets giving way to more open routes the further out you go. These roads can be quieter than the M6, which sometimes means a longer wait for other traffic to pass if you need to flag someone down, so it's worth calling us as soon as it's safe to do so rather than waiting to see if the problem resolves itself.</p>

          <div class="callout">
            <p><strong>Broken down on the edge of town or on a quieter lane near Walsall?</strong> Give us the nearest neighbourhood, junction or a landmark you can see, since postcodes can cover a wide area on the borough's fringes.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

